<h2>Why ice melt damages concrete &mdash; and it is not what most people think</h2>

<p>The usual assumption is that the chemical eats the concrete. Mostly it does
not. The damage is physical, and it is called freeze-thaw scaling.</p>

<p>Ice melt works by lowering the freezing point of water, which produces
liquid brine on your slab. That brine soaks into the pores in the concrete. When
the temperature drops again overnight, the water in those pores freezes and
expands by about nine percent. Repeat that cycle a few dozen times over a winter
and the surface starts flaking away in thin sheets. That is scaling.</p>

<p>Three things make it worse. <strong>Young concrete</strong> is the big one:
a slab poured within the last year has not reached full strength and is far more
vulnerable, which is why most manufacturers say to keep de-icers off new
concrete for a full winter. <strong>Concrete that was not air-entrained</strong>
has no microscopic voids for expanding ice to escape into. And
<strong>saturated concrete</strong> &mdash; a slab that drains badly and stays
wet &mdash; has more water in it to freeze in the first place.</p>

<p>Calcium chloride and magnesium chloride pull in more moisture than rock salt
does, which can mean more freeze-thaw cycling on a slab that never gets a chance
to dry. On sound, mature, well-drained concrete, all of them are manageable. On a
slab poured last spring, none of them is a good idea.</p>

<h2>What "pet-safe" means, and what it does not</h2>

<p>There is no regulatory definition of pet-safe ice melt, so treat it as a
description rather than a guarantee. Products carrying the label are generally
built on magnesium chloride or CMA and avoid sodium chloride, because the
problems for animals are mechanical and gastric rather than acute poisoning.</p>

<p>Coarse crystals lodge between toe pads and cause cuts; salt left on paws
irritates skin; and a dog that licks its paws afterwards ingests the product,
which is where the stomach upset comes from. Milder chemistry helps with all
three, but the most effective steps are physical: spread less, sweep up the
residue once the ice has released, and wipe paws at the door.</p>

<h2>Spreading less, and spreading it earlier</h2>

<p>Ice melt is not there to melt a storm away. Its job is to break the bond
between ice and pavement so you can lift the ice off with a shovel. That means
a thin, even scatter is the correct application, not a heavy layer.</p>

<p>Two habits cut usage sharply. Spread <em>before</em> a storm, so ice never
bonds to the surface in the first place &mdash; you will use a fraction of what
it takes to burn through afterwards. And shovel first, then treat what is left:
clearing snow mechanically before reaching for a bag is the single biggest
saving available, and it keeps chloride out of the soil at the edges of the
walk, where it accumulates and kills the grass over a few seasons.</p>

